1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,500
 So now let's talk about the integration of Amazon bedrock and a service called CloudWatch.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:10,020
 So CloudWatch is a way for you to do cloud monitoring.

3
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:13,720
 So CloudWatch has many services but you can have metrics,

4
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:18,460
 you can have alarms, you can have logs and so on in CloudWatch and you them all.

5
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:22,020
 And many services and areas have integration with CloudWatch.

6
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:26,340
 So for Amazon bedrock, what you can do is you can do model invocation logging.

7
00:00:26,340 --> 00:00:28,020
 And that's something that can come up at the exam.

8
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:31,560
 So the idea is that you want to send all the invocations,

9
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:39,760
 so all the inputs and the outputs of model invocations into either CloudWatch logs or Amazon history.

10
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:44,480
 And this can include the text, the images as well as the embeddings.

11
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:48,780
 And this is very helpful because you get a history of everything that happened within bedrock.

12
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:53,180
 On top of it, you can analyze the data further and build alerting on top of it.

13
00:00:53,180 --> 00:01:00,100
 Thanks to CloudWatch logs inside, which is a service which allows you to analyze the logs in real time from CloudWatch logs.

14
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:05,860
 So the idea here is that we get full tracing and monitoring of bedrock thanks to CloudWatch logs.

15
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:08,780
 The other one is CloudWatch metrics.

16
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:14,500
 So the idea is that Amazon bedrock is going to publish a lot of different metrics to CloudWatch.

17
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:18,060
 And then you can appear in CloudWatch metrics.

18
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:24,540
 And some of them may be for general usage of bedrock, but some of them may also be related to guardrails.

19
00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:32,180
 So there's one called content filter count, which helps you understand if some content was filtered from a guardrail.

20
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:36,500
 And so we can do with it is that once you have these kind of metrics in CloudWatch metrics,

21
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:40,580
 you can build CloudWatch alarms on top of them to get alerted, for example,

22
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:49,260
 when something is caught by a guardrail or when Amazon bedrock is exceeding a specific threshold for a specific metric.

23
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:57,180
 So model invocation logging and CloudWatch metrics are very important in Amazon bedrock and there are topics that can appear in the exam.

24
00:01:57,180 --> 00:01:59,900
 So I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

